{
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "overview",
      "title": "The book in brief",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "important",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Knowledge work is slow because it waits, and the waiting is governed by how much work is in progress and how busy people are.",
              "cites": [
                "ch01/s01/p002",
                "ch02/s01/p001",
                "ch03/s01/p004"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The book starts from a measurement: most of an item's delivery time is spent waiting, not being worked on. ",
              "cites": [
                "ch01/s01/p002"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Faster people or longer hours cannot fix a delay that happens while nobody is working.",
              "cites": [
                "ch01/s02/p003"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Two pieces of queueing arithmetic explain the waiting. ",
              "cites": [
                "ch02/s01/p001"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Little's law ties work in progress to time in the system, so more WIP at the same throughput means longer waits; ",
              "cites": [
                "ch02/s01/p001",
                "ch02/s01/p005"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "the utilization curve shows waiting staying modest to about 70% and exploding past 80%, made worse by variability.",
              "cites": [
                "ch03/s01/p004",
                "ch03/s02/p003"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The middle of the book turns the mechanism into levers and tests them: WIP limits cut average cycle time from 31 to about 10 days with flat throughput, ",
              "cites": [
                "ch04/s02/t001",
                "ch04/s02/p005"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "and smaller batches shorten waits for the same reason.",
              "cites": [
                "ch05/s02/p003"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It closes by finding the queues nobody measures — approvals, hand-offs, work turned away at a limit — ",
              "cites": [
                "ch06/s01/p001",
                "ch06/s02/p004"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "and by recasting management as tending the flow of work rather than the busyness of people.",
              "cites": [
                "ch07/s01/p001"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "Forecast delivery from WIP and throughput instead of effort estimates.",
                "cites": [
                  "ch02/s02/p002"
                ]
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Choose a WIP limit and a slack level for a team, and defend them.",
                "cites": [
                  "ch04/s01/p002",
                  "ch03/s03/p006"
                ]
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Find and shorten the hidden queues in your own process.",
                "cites": [
                  "ch06/s02/p004"
                ]
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
